d":null,"_score_spread":null,"_gate_roic_consistency":null,"_gp_roic_consistency":null,"_score_roic_consistency":null,"_gate_incr_roic":null,"_gp_incr_roic":null,"_score_incr_roic":null,"_gate_margin_advantage":null,"_gp_margin_advantage":null,"_score_margin_advantage":null,"_gate_pool_share":-0.04522090027150871,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":21.9,"_gate_rev_durability":0.10875391790890743,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":79.4,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":null,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.13720590622607665,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":100.0,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.0644678872875668,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":100.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.02187985499741067,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":63.5,"_gate_insider_own":0.37602,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":0.012,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":1.2,"pf":[],"description_full":"Credit Acceptance Corporation engages in the provision of financing programs, and related products and services in the United States. It advances money to automobile dealers in exchange for the right to service the underlying consumer loans; and buys the consumer loans from the dealers and keeps the amount collected from the consumers. The company is also involved in the business of reinsuring coverage under vehicle service contracts sold to consumers by dealers on vehicles financed by the company. It serves independent and franchised automobile dealers. The company was founded in 1972 and is headquartered in Southfield, Michigan.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Vinayak R. Hegde | CEO, President & Director | Age 55 | Compensation: $934,047 (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $1.0M ranks in the above the sector median among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 11%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $455K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $22K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Credit Acceptance Corporation is a mid-cap financial services business with a $5.1B market cap on $2.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +8% over the past three years and +11% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 39% against a 10% cost of capital \u2014 a 28-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $1.1B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 45 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.49\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 2.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 13.7% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 38% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and within its sector the business captures 1.7\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.3) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (31.2% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins softening -1.6pp YoY \u2014 early warning on the moat","sev":"amber","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Heavy insider selling (net $20.0M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-20037971.09},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 15% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 34% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Financial Services peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"(CACC) Movement as an Input in Quant Signal Sets","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/149/CACC_Movement_as_an_Input_in_Quant_Signal_Sets_111026082803_1794317283.html","date":"2026-11-10","timestamp":1794317283.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.681,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Is Quarterly Net Loss Altering The Investment Case For Credit Acceptance (CACC)?","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/diversified-financials/nasdaq-cacc/credit-acceptance/news/is-quarterly-net-loss-alte